1
00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:09.240
[S01] The older I get, the less I judge. When I was a kid walking down the street and I would see the homeless man that was acting crazy, I would look at that guy and be like, that guy's weird.

2
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:17.879
[S01] And now I look at that guy and I'm like, he might be on to something. He might actually be the only one that is acting reasonable here.

3
00:00:18.179 --> 00:00:26.460
[S01] In this fake world, there are fake people, fake jobs, fake identities, you know, uh, fake pursuits.

4
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:32.070
[S01] Like, everything is so manufactured and artificial that you sort of go crazy.

5
00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:38.250
[S01] And when you're a kid, you're kind of, you're like too young and too naive to understand everything.

6
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:45.149
[S01] But as I've gotten older, especially into my, you know, first few years into adulthood, I've realized no one understands what's going on.

7
00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:51.539
[S01] And everyone's just pretending they understand what's going on. And that is what drives me crazy because it's all so fake. People are fake.

8
00:00:52.049 --> 00:00:57.119
[S01] You know, it's like the, the jobs that you work, it's like, what do you actually do?

9
00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:00.719
[S01] Like when you, when you sit down and you look at like, what is the actual work you're doing?

10
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:08.370
[S01] You can't help but wonder, okay, what is the impact on the world that this has? Like, why do I have to do this to, to, you know, put food on the table?

11
00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:12.560
[S01] Like, it's just, nothing makes sense. There's a term called cath gas. I've used it on my channel before.

12
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:21.689
[S01] But cath gas is basically when things are unreasonably difficult and are like, become very, um,

13
00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:29.879
[S01] basically, they become confusing and complex for no reason. It's like going to the DMV.

14
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:36.150
[S01] It's like, why do I got to jump through all these hoops to do such a basic thing? Now, apply that to like all concepts of life.

15
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:44.730
[S01] I feel like when you go to, um, what drives me the most crazy is socially, when you go to some sort of public event and you're around other people socially.

16
00:01:44.849 --> 00:01:51.060
[S01] Everyone is like, uh, it's like, mm, yeah, I'm doing great. How are you, Cheryl?

17
00:01:51.239 --> 00:01:56.280
[S01] Hmm, I'm also doing good. You know, the kids are good. The family's good. It's like, what are we talking about?

18
00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.239
[S01] What, what is the conversation? What is it? What is the actual density of which?

19
00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:07.439
[S01] In, in the topics that we're covering, it's just nothingness. Everything is nothingness. Nothing makes sense. And I know I'm being very vague, but that's intentional here.

20
00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:11.819
[S01] It's like, what in life, um, really has depth anymore?

21
00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:19.710
[S01] And the reason nothing has depth is because most people are miserable. Most people have no idea what's going on. Most people can't conceptualize things.

22
00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:25.770
[S01] And so the cope is to just, they have the surface level for everything. And because of that, you, we, we're just disconnected.

23
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:32.009
[S01] I mean, you're disconnected. It's basically like, be fake for the sake of fitting in or, you know,

24
00:02:32.219 --> 00:02:36.240
[S01] in any way, shape or form, try to act reasonably and then people think that you're crazy.

25
00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:42.409
[S01] Uh, this is kind of a tired talking point, to be honest. It's, it's been talked about many times before. It's like when Kanye West said,

26
00:02:42.659 --> 00:02:45.870
[S01] I just tell the truth and telling truth in a world full of lies is crazy.

27
00:02:46.469 --> 00:02:52.590
[S01] You know, or people talk about how like, maybe the schizophrenic people know what's going on more than us.

28
00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.379
[S01] Maybe the mentally ill are actually the mentally sane ones.

29
00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:01.740
[S01] Um, and, and like, this has been a talking point that's, you know, been said over and over again, but

30
00:03:02.219 --> 00:03:05.520
[S01] the very fact that it has means there's some basis of truth to it.

31
00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:13.560
[S01] I think everyone has a limit. Everyone has a limit for how much they can put up with. And some people, it's just lower than others. My threshold is very low,

32
00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:19.169
[S01] which is not a good thing because it doesn't really play to my favor. But, you know, I'll go into,

33
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:27.870
[S01] I told this story, but like, I think it was like seven months ago, I went to like this job interview, uh, for a corporate job and I was still in school at the time.

34
00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:35.520
[S01] And I had to go and meet with these HR people and it was like, I was in a, I was in a call with like three other middle aged women.

35
00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:42.719
[S01] And a bro, I could not handle the fakeness. So I literally just blatantly addressed it like very directly. I'm like, hey,

36
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:48.180
[S01] I don't mean to be rude, but if we could just, um, get to the point of this meeting and I'll move on.

37
00:03:48.419 --> 00:03:53.550
[S01] Hey, that would be, that would work perfect for me because they're doing this whole like fake, like pretending they care about you.

38
00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:57.990
[S01] And HR is the fakest fricking job on earth and it's just full of faking. Oh my gosh.

39
00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.840
[S01] I'm kind of spiraling here, talking about a lot of different things, but I, I, I just understand it. I understand why people go crazy.

40
00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:08.219
[S01] Because what percentage of your day to day activities are

41
00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:16.680
[S01] in any way, shape or form, like based in logic? It, it's kind of just like, life is so absurd that we try to fill the void

42
00:04:17.009 --> 00:04:23.699
[S01] of our existence with doing anything because anything distracts us from facing or confronting the fact that life is absurd.

43
00:04:23.939 --> 00:04:26.850
[S01] And so we just, we're just, we just go insane, bro.

44
00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:36.329
[S01] Um, and, and I think there, a scary realization that I've had is that, is that people can be pushed a lot further than they think.

45
00:04:36.569 --> 00:04:41.160
[S01] It's kind of like in running, this is like, think of it in, in a metaphor of a guy who's running.

46
00:04:41.389 --> 00:04:47.970
[S01] It's like, you know, you can, someone in their head is like, oh, I can only run, you know, three miles before I start walking.

47
00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.579
[S01] But then it's like, when you're at three miles, can you take one step further? Just one step? It's like, yeah, I mean,

48
00:04:53.329 --> 00:04:57.629
[S01] I can take one step further. And then it's like, once you take that step, well, can you take one more step?

49
00:04:57.629 --> 00:04:59.069
[S01] And it's like, yeah, now apply this.

50
00:04:59.879 --> 00:05:04.709
[S02] On and on and on, and obviously that's a metaphor, but in real life, it's like, it's like in a toxic relationship.

51
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.029
[S02] You can sort of put up with one red flag and that's okay.

52
00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.750
[S02] And then it turns to two red flags and like, eh, that's okay, three red flags.

53
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.709
[S02] All the, all of a sudden, you're at the point of the, what's called the sunken cost fallacy,

54
00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:17.949
[S02] which is where you're so deep into something that it makes no sense to, to leave now.

55
00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.470
[S02] Cause you'll, you'll lose everything that you've built.

56
00:05:20.639 --> 00:05:21.990
[S02] And so that's kind of what life is.

57
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.990
[S02] It's like you work a corporate job that you absolutely hate.

58
00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:31.149
[S02] And you do it for five years because you think you're going to get promoted and you're hoping, you're hoping that this new promotion

59
00:05:31.319 --> 00:05:34.670
[S02] is going to be a position that you work less and you get paid more.

60
00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:37.269
[S02] But then it's not, so you're like, well, I've already dedicated five years.

61
00:05:37.279 --> 00:05:39.149
[S02] So what's two more years to then get promoted again?

62
00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:42.910
[S02] And you get stuck in this trap and then it's like, well, you know, the 401k, I'm starting to add to that.

63
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:44.910
[S02] There's some benefits that I can keep if I stay here.

64
00:05:45.079 --> 00:05:46.269
[S02] And that's just life.

65
00:05:46.959 --> 00:05:51.870
[S02] You know, it's just like you stick in things that you don't want to be in for the sake of thinking that,

66
00:05:51.959 --> 00:05:56.149
[S02] you know, the light at the end of the tunnel is right around the corner, but then it never is, right?

67
00:05:56.199 --> 00:05:58.589
[S02] The goalpost is always moved and that's life.

68
00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.509
[S02] And that's literally why people go insane.

69
00:06:00.519 --> 00:06:02.949
[S02] It's because the goalpost keeps on moving and moving and moving.

70
00:06:02.959 --> 00:06:07.230
[S02] And it's a perpetual state of being and it's exhausting, bro.

71
00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:14.230
[S02] I think like modern life, it just constantly demands your attention, energy, uh, your emotional bandwidth.

72
00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:18.350
[S02] It just requires you to put up with BS constantly.

73
00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:25.709
[S02] And then you have financial pressure, you have loneliness, you have to deal with the uncertainty that comes with life and being able to live like

74
00:06:25.959 --> 00:06:30.670
[S02] in, in terms of providing for yourself and your family, there's burnout.

75
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.790
[S02] All these things just accumulate.

76
00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:37.870
[S02] And it's like, what is going to happen to the psyche of an individual who just takes on too much burden?

77
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.430
[S02] Because by the way, I don't know a single person that doesn't deal with the burden in their life.

78
00:06:41.519 --> 00:06:46.750
[S02] Even when you have no burdens, even like nepo babies that like live like these luxurious lives.

79
00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:51.910
[S02] The way that your brain works is like, it needs a, the stimuli of stress.

80
00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.029
[S02] Because that's how it's been for millennia.

81
00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.189
[S02] Like you, oh, there's always been threats, there's always been things.

82
00:06:56.199 --> 00:07:01.110
[S02] So if you don't have that, then you start to stress out over very simple things that to other people is a walk in the park.

83
00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.029
[S02] You know, it's, it's, it's nothing.

84
00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:09.550
[S02] And then all of this basically leads you to feel disconnected from others because your problems are so isolated to just you.

85
00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.709
[S02] It's like there, it's a very personal experience.

86
00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:17.069
[S02] And before you know it, I mean, you're depressed, you're anxious and you, you don't know what to do.

87
00:07:17.079 --> 00:07:18.389
[S02] And there's constant pressure.

88
00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:23.509
[S02] And what's scary is I'm only 22 years old and I've, you know, gotten a glimpse into what it looks like.

89
00:07:23.519 --> 00:07:30.269
[S02] But I've seen people, I've known people much older than me who, I mean, they're just barely hanging on by a thread.

90
00:07:30.269 --> 00:07:34.509
[S02] But once again, it's like, it, it's that fallacy of like,

91
00:07:35.399 --> 00:07:37.029
[S02] you can always take one more step.

92
00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.350
[S02] They can always put up with just a little bit more and they're always scared to take that leap of faith.

93
00:07:40.519 --> 00:07:41.509
[S02] And that's the life.

94
00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:45.709
[S02] That's why there, there was a quote a few years back that was very viral.

95
00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:49.670
[S02] Um, and it said something like, most men live lives of quiet desperation.

96
00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:55.389
